# Contributing from this fork

This checkout is `thomasbrugman/t3code`, a fork of `pingdotgg/t3code`. `CONTRIBUTING.md` and
`AGENTS.md` are the rules. Architecture context is `.cursor/architecture.md`, which only points at
`docs/internals/overview.md`.

Keep everything under `.cursor/` on this fork. Do not include it in a pull request to
`pingdotgg/t3code`.

## Commands

Use a login shell so `vp` selects the Node version in `package.json` (`engines.node`, currently
26.12.1). pnpm is `packageManager` (currently 12.14.0).

- Install: `vp i`. Unattended installs use `vp i --frozen-lockfile -- --yes`, as
  `scripts/setup-worktree.ts` does.
- App: `vp run dev`. Open the pairing URL it prints. Leave `VITE_HTTP_URL` and `VITE_WS_URL` unset.
- One test: `vp test run <files>`. Narrow a large file with `-t "<name>"`.
- Lint paths: `vp lint <files>`.
- One package's types: `vp run --filter <package> typecheck`.
- Checks for the files this branch changed: `vp run check:changed`.

`docs/operations/development.md` is the command reference. Repo-wide `vp check`, `vp run typecheck`,
and `vp run test` belong to CI unless someone asks for them.

## Upstream pull requests

Sync `main` from `https://github.com/pingdotgg/t3code.git`, branch from that `main`, and open the
product PR against `pingdotgg/t3code`. Fork-only setup, including `.cursor/`, stays on this fork.

```sh
git remote add upstream https://github.com/pingdotgg/t3code.git # once
git fetch upstream
git checkout main
git merge upstream/main
git push origin main
git checkout -b fix/short-name
```

From `CONTRIBUTING.md`:

- Upstream is not seeking features. Focused bug fixes, reliability, performance, and maintenance
  are the changes most likely to be reviewed. Opening a PR does not guarantee review.
- One underlying problem per PR. Contract, server, client, test, and doc edits for that problem
  stay together.
- Features and intentional behavior changes need prior maintainer approval of the direction and
  scope, and the PR links that approval. A very small obvious bug fix, or a focused option on an
  existing capability, can use the exceptions written in that guide.
- Explain how the problem was established and how the change was checked. UI changes need
  before/after screenshots uploaded to GitHub, not committed.
- Conventional commit titles, in plain language, such as `fix(web): new threads no longer spike CPU`.
  When an agent wrote the change, end the PR body with the model and harness.
- PRs that state a Gemini model, or Muse Wonder other than Muse Wonder Full, wrote the code are closed.
